	abstract = {If{\textbackslash}\{(X\_i, Y\_i){\textbackslash}\}\_\{i=1\}{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}infty\}is a sequence of independent identically distributed discrete random pairs with(X\_i, Y\_i) {\textasciitilde} p(x,y), Slepian and Wolf have shown that theXprocess and theYprocess can be separately described to a common receiver at ratesR\_XandR\_Yhits per symbol ifR\_X + R\_Y {\textgreater} H(X,Y), R\_X {\textgreater} H(X{\textbackslash}midY), R\_Y {\textgreater} H(Y{\textbackslash}midX). A simpler proof of this result will be given. As a consequence it is established that the Slepian-Wolf theorem is true without change for arbitrary ergodic processes{\textbackslash}\{(X\_i,Y\_i){\textbackslash}\}\_\{i=1\}{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}infty\}and countably infinite alphabets. The extension to an arbitrary number of processes is immediate.},
